          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Postnikov system
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+factorization+system"} infinitary factorization system
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n-epi%2C+n-mono%29+factorization+system"} (n-epi, n-mono)-factorizations
            through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-images"} n-images
            in an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            The basic (and historically first) example is the factorization in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of morphisms to the point: here the Postnikov system assigns to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tower"} tower
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 3
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \to \cdots \to X_3 \to X_2 \to X_1 \to X_0  \simeq *
          %p
            where each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_k
            is a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy (k-2)-type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truncated+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (n-2)-truncated object
            and such that each morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{k+1} \to X_k
            induces an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            in degree
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq k-2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            into its “layers” as seen by the degree of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+classes"} characteristic classes
            which give the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+extension"} ∞-group extension
            in each layer are called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+invariants"} Postnikov invariants
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-invariants"} k-invariants
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            More generally, in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            and for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
            the corresponding Postnikov system is a
