          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            In full generality, the Postnikov system is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+factorization+system"} infinitary factorization system
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n-epi%2C+n-mono%29+factorization+system"} (n-epi, n-mono)-factorizations
            through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-images"} n-images
            in an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            The following spells this out explicitly for default
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            and in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presentation
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} model structure on topological spaces
            and the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#DefinitionForTopologicalSpaces"} For topological spaces
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "#DefinitionForSimplicialSets"} For simplicial sets
                \.
          %p
            With a little bit of care this induces corresponding presentations of Postnikov systems in general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            by prolonging to suitable
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structures on simplicial presheaves
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#DefinitionForSimplicialPresheaves"} For simplicial presheaves
              \.
          %p For more discussion of the general abstract situation see at
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+tower+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} Postnikov tower in an (∞,1)-category
          %h3#DefinitionForTopologicalSpaces For topological spaces
          %p
            Historically, Postnikov systems were first described on the model of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            constituted by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %p
            A
            %strong Postnikov system
            or
            %strong
              Postnikov
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tower"} tower
            or
            %strong Moore-Postnikov tower/system
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+connected+topological+space"} path connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
